meta {
  name: 11 - Issues Ascending Sweep
  type: graphql
  seq: 11
}

post {
  url: {{base_url}}
  body: graphql
  auth: inherit
}

body:graphql {
  query IssuesAscendingSweep {
    titleAsc:  issues(first: 4, sort: [{title: {order: Ascending}}])  { nodes { title updatedAt } }
    titleDesc: issues(first: 4, sort: [{title: {order: Descending}}]) { nodes { title updatedAt } }
    updatedAsc: issues(
      first: 250
      includeArchived: true
      sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]
      filter: {updatedAt: {gt: "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"}}
    ) {
      nodes { id updatedAt }
      pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
    }
  }
}

docs {
  Reference: https://linear.app/developers/pagination

  Establishes the ASCENDING sweep that rescues per-page checkpointing for this stream.

  `04 - OrderBy Direction Probe` proved `orderBy` is descending-only, which would force
  end-of-sweep checkpointing everywhere. But `02 - Introspect Pagination Args` found a
  second, richer ordering argument on `issues` / `projects` / `initiatives`:
  `sort: [IssueSortInput!]`, whose `updatedAt` member is an `UpdatedAtSort` with an
  explicit `order: PaginationSortOrder` enum of `{Ascending, Descending}` (plus a `nulls`
  placement control). This is the escape hatch — it gives an explicitly ASCENDING
  `updatedAt` sweep, so the connector can safely checkpoint after every page.

  **Round 1 (2026-07-30) — mechanism only, via a non-tied key.** Every `updatedAt` in the
  workspace was identical then, so `sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]` and its
  `Descending` twin returned the same node order — inconclusive, not evidence of a broken
  argument. The `titleAsc` / `titleDesc` aliases use `title`, which IS distinct, and showed
  an exact reversal. That proved `PaginationSortOrder` is genuinely applied, but left the
  actual question open.

  **Round 2 (2026-08-04) — ASCENDING `updatedAt` CONFIRMED.** `Seeding/D1` bumped EST-1's
  `updatedAt` from `2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z` to `2026-08-04T18:12:51.985Z`, breaking the
  workspace-wide tie. `updatedAsc` now returns EST-1 **last**, after the three rows still
  carrying the bulk-seed timestamp — newest last, i.e. genuinely ascending. Cross-checked
  against `orderBy: updatedAt` (descending), which puts EST-1 first.

  **This is the evidence per-page checkpointing rests on** for Issues, Projects and
  Initiatives: in ascending order an updated row's `updatedAt` only increases, so it moves
  LATER in the sweep and can never slip behind the read position. Labels cannot use `sort`
  (`42 - Labels Sort Rejected`) and therefore cannot have this guarantee.

  Round 2 also confirmed ordinary field mutations DO bump `updatedAt` — it is specifically
  ARCHIVAL that does not (`14 - Issues Include Archived`).

  **Incidental finding in the saved example: `titleAsc` / `titleDesc` return only 3 rows,
  not 4.** They deliberately omit `includeArchived: true`, so the archived EST-2 ("Connect
  your tools") silently drops out — an unplanned but clean re-demonstration that the default
  hides the archived partition, visible right next to the 4-row `updatedAsc` alias that does
  pass the flag.

  `updatedAsc` is the exact sweep query the connector will issue for backfill and for
  incremental (only the `filter.updatedAt` bound differs; the plan uses `gte`, not the `gt`
  shown here — see `07 - Cursor Filter Boundary` for why).

  **Second observed finding — `sort` and `orderBy` return DIFFERENT cursor encodings.**
  Under `orderBy` the `endCursor` is the raw node UUID (see `12 - Issues Pagination Walk`);
  under `sort` it becomes a base64 blob. From this run's saved example:

      endCursor = "eyJrZXkiOiJhZGJjN2RlYi02NjA3LTRmMzktYWRlMi1jMjMyNjhkYzNlYzciLCJncm91cHMiOlsiIiwiIl19"

  which base64-decodes to:

      {"key":"adbc7deb-6607-4f39-ade2-c23268dc3ec7","groups":["",""]}

  **This is not an opaque server-issued token, and the distinction matters.** The decoded
  payload is composed entirely of values derived from the document itself — `key` is the
  last node's `id`, `groups` holds that node's sort-key values. There is no server-side
  continuation state, no session handle, and nothing with a wall-clock lifetime, so it is
  exempt from the opaque-token durability burden (no TTL to expire, nothing for unrelated
  provider-side writes to invalidate). It is a keyset cursor wearing a base64 coat.

  Regardless, the connector must NOT checkpoint this value across sync cycles. `after` is
  used only WITHIN a single sweep; what gets persisted between cycles is `updatedAt`. That
  keeps the design independent of Linear's cursor encoding, which — as this finding shows
  — silently changes shape depending on which ordering argument you pass.
}

example {
  name: 200 — ASCENDING updatedAt CONFIRMED (newest last, post-seed)

  request: {
    url: https://api.linear.app/graphql
    method: POST
    mode: graphql

    body:graphql: {
      query IssuesAscendingSweep {
        titleAsc:  issues(first: 4, sort: [{title: {order: Ascending}}])  { nodes { title updatedAt } }
        titleDesc: issues(first: 4, sort: [{title: {order: Descending}}]) { nodes { title updatedAt } }
        updatedAsc: issues(
          first: 250
          includeArchived: true
          sort: [{updatedAt: {order: Ascending}}]
          filter: {updatedAt: {gt: "2020-01-01T00:00:00Z"}}
        ) {
          nodes { id updatedAt }
          pageInfo { hasNextPage endCursor }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  response: {
    headers: {
      x-complexity: 8
      x-ratelimit-requests-limit: 2500
      x-ratelimit-requests-remaining: 2490
      x-ratelimit-complexity-limit: 3000000
      x-ratelimit-complexity-remaining: 2999992
    }

    status: {
      code: 200
      text: OK
    }

    body: {
      type: json
      content: '''
        {
          "data": {
            "titleAsc": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "title": "Get familiar with Linear",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T18:12:51.985Z"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Import your data",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Set up your teams",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                }
              ]
            },
            "titleDesc": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "title": "Set up your teams",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Import your data",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Get familiar with Linear",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T18:12:51.985Z"
                }
              ]
            },
            "updatedAsc": {
              "nodes": [
                {
                  "id": "431c9f95-fe23-44cb-9391-f4880c51bede",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "id": "86eeb50c-332c-46a7-bebf-a0b67dc346bf",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "id": "adbc7deb-6607-4f39-ade2-c23268dc3ec7",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-07-30T21:35:03.808Z"
                },
                {
                  "id": "4937bc7e-6bda-4612-9a80-3fbaaec872d9",
                  "updatedAt": "2026-08-04T18:12:51.985Z"
                }
              ],
              "pageInfo": {
                "hasNextPage": false,
                "endCursor": "eyJrZXkiOiI0OTM3YmM3ZS02YmRhLTQ2MTItOWE4MC0zZmJhYWVjODcyZDkiLCJncm91cHMiOlsiIiwiIl19"
              }
            }
          }
        }
      '''
    }
  }
}
